WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.380
Avengers: Endgame almost saw Bruce Banner
face off against arguably his greatest foe...

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.100
himself.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.500
In a recent interview, co-writers Christopher
Markus and Stephen McFeely revealed that they

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.619
scripted a scene in which Banner meets his
angry, green alter-ego in a Soul Stone-induced

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:29.880
vision after using the Stark Gauntlet to bring
back all of the dusted heroes.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.900
The scene, unfortunately, never made it before
the cameras.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:38.020
The scene followed a rule of sorts established
in Avengers: Infinity War, in the poignant

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.429
scene in which Thanos encounters young Gamora
inside the Soul Stone after snapping away

00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:44.360
half of all life in the universe.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.100
"Did you do it?

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:46.500
"...Yes."

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:47.760
"What did it cost?"

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.280
"Everything."

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.499
Thanks to Endgame's titanic run time, however,
the "rule" was never invoked again; a scene

00:00:58.499 --> 00:01:02.929
was shot in which Tony Stark has a Soul Stone-based
conversation with an older version of his

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:07.480
daughter Morgan after he uses the Infinity
Stones to dust Thanos and his army, but it

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.080
ended up on the cutting room floor.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.400
The scene in which Banner and Hulk hash out
their differences after the restorative snap

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.850
existed in an early draft of the script, according
to Markus.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:17.840
The scribe recalled:

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.460
"We did actually write a version, and this
existed before the Ancient One scene was slowly

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.110
beaten out, but there was a version where
it was Banner talking to The Hulk.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.300
And we never shot it, but it was kind of a
cool thing to get them both on screen together

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.320
interacting."

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:36.620
We disagree: it would have been a really cool
thing, but when you're crafting the epic conclusion

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.140
to a decade-long saga involving dozens of
characters, you simply can't include every

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.170
single cool idea you might have.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.220
In fact, it probably won't surprise you to
know that the Banner/Hulk sequence was far

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.420
from the only one to be scripted for Endgame,
only to get cut before the cameras rolled.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.620
"Dude, you're embarrassing me in front of
the wizards."

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.820
After the devastating events of Infinity War,
Markus and McFeely used Endgame's five-year

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.140
time jump to explore the effects that such
a brutal loss would have on the Avengers.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.620
They all took interesting, and often unexpected,
directions.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.170
Banner merged the two halves of himself to
permanently become Smart Hulk, Hawkeye became

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:21.440
a bloodthirsty vigilante, and Thor got drunk
and bro-y, for example.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.500
"Listen buddy, if you don't log off this game
immediately, I'm gonna fly over to your house,

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.159
come down to that basement you're hiding in,
rip off your arms, and shove them up your

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:29.660
butt!"

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.939
But in an early draft of the screenplay, one
team member — James Rhodes, AKA War Machine

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.640
— made a pretty big leap from superheroism
to the highest levels of politics.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.739
In a recent chat with ComicBook.com, Markus
explained:

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.099
"We took it out because it didn't have any
story weight, but I believe that during one

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.980
draft, in the five year jump, Rhodey became
vice president."

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.069
It actually makes a lot of sense.

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:56.720
He was a decorated career soldier before suiting
up, he was pro-registration during the events

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.349
of Captain America: Civil War, and he's so
darn patriotic that he was briefly referred

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:02.940
to as the Iron Patriot.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.080
Heck, Rhodey could step out of the MCU and
into our universe to run for office, and he'd

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:08.940
have our vote.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.499
Another scene involved a character that Marvel
fans have been waiting patiently to see onscreen

00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:16.450
forever: Richard Rider, the man called Nova.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.180
In the comics, Rider is an Earthling tapped
by Rhomann Dey, the last survivor of the destruction

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.120
of Xandar, to house the entirety of that planet's
knowledge — the "Xandarian Worldmind" — and

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.450
the combined power of its armed forces, the
Nova Corps.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:30.959
Xandar, Nova Corps.

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:36.439
leader Nova Prime, and Dey were all introduced
in 2014's Guardians of the Galaxy, but Rider

00:03:36.439 --> 00:03:40.349
was not — and if the character had been
included in Endgame, his backstory would have

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:41.919
been significantly tweaked.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.420
Also, it would have been he, and not Hulk,
who would have come crashing to Earth to warn

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.820
the Avengers of Thanos' impending arrival
in Infinity War.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.650
Markus told ComicBook.com:

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:56.019
"I remember in the big manifesto thing that
we did, where the first work we did on these

00:03:56.019 --> 00:03:57.639
movies where we just...

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:02.140
every possible storyline that you could take
from what's around, that Nova has the Xandarian

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:02.640
Worldmind.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.940
In this conception, he would be the herald,
not unlike the Hulk... that one member of

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.120
the Nova Corps [who] survived Thanos' attack.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.060
The Xandarian Worldmind would be the voice
of Glenn Close, because you had her, and why

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:14.560
not?

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.500
And that he would be Richard Rider and he
would come to Earth... it would've been amazing."

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.560
Another choice that was kicked around early
in the scripting process would have altered

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.150
Endgame's narrative even more drastically.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:29.390
In a wide-ranging sitdown with the New York
Times, the ace writing duo explained that

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:34.200
originally, the Avengers' time travel shenanigans
didn't involve a return to the Battle of New

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:34.700
York.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.360
Instead, the team would have wound up in Asgard,
where an invisible, stealth-suit-wearing Tony

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.460
Stark would've done battle with Heimdall,
who would've been able to see him.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:48.200
Look, we understand that massive projects
like this go through lengthy bouts of revision,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.970
and ultimately, Markus and McFeely did an
amazing job of crafting the complex and crowded

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.920
narratives of Infinity War and Endgame.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.520
But invisible Iron Man fighting Heimdall?

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.680
Of all the cool things the scribes were forced
to cut, this has got to be the coolest.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.510
If the producers of the upcoming Disney+ series
What If...? haven't been made aware that this

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.720
was once on the table, someone needs to clue
them in, pronto.

